From 3a5ecf3ac04e4a22f8bfad7b89d231b1e4a1cb03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TorrServerManager <noreply@torrservermanager.local>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 20:10:56 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] gstreamer: take turns on a torrent instead of competing
 for its connections
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

TorrServer creates a reader per HTTP request and its cache splits the torrent's
connection budget across every open reader (ConnectionsLimit / len(readers)), giving
each one its own fetch-this-piece-now claim at its own offset. Probing, reading the
cue index and prerolling a pipeline each open a reader, so running them together
starves whichever one the viewer is waiting for — a pipeline can fail its five second
state-change budget while the swarm is plainly fast.

Serialise them behind a per-torrent gate. Per torrent, not per file, because the
budget is shared per torrent: probing one episode competes with playing another from
the same pack. Requests wait on their own context; callers without one give up after a
timeout and proceed, since stalling behind another operation would be worse than the
contention.

Also debounce repeated segment failures: a failing segment fails the same way when
asked again immediately, and players retry hard enough to show four attempts inside
two seconds, each costing a seek and a fresh reader. Replay the last error briefly
instead. Cancellation is not remembered — it says nothing about the segment.
